<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chu Ci</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Songs of Chu, Chuci, Elegies of Chu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chu Ci (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> of Chu) is an anthology of Chinese poetry compiled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, in its received form by Wang Yi in the second century CE. It contains the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-sao">Li Sao</a> and other poems attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qu-yuan">Qu Yuan</a> of the State of Chu, the shamanistic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-songs">Nine Songs</a>, and later works in the same style. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry">Book of Songs</a> it is one of the two founding collections of Chinese poetry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chu-ci.jpg" alt="Chu Ci" width="320" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chen Hongshou · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qu_Yuan_Sang_while_Walking.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Chu Ci takes its name from a form of poetry that began in the State of Chu, in what is now central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, then on the southern fringe of the Chinese cultural area. Chu blended the culture of the Central Plain with influences associated with the south, and a Chinese form of shamanism was prominent there. Many of the verses describe spirit journeys, encounters with fragrant plants, spirits and deities, and travel to the heavens, the ends of the earth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and the mythical Mount Kunlun.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qu-yuan">Qu Yuan</a> is the central figure of the collection. According to common tradition he was an official at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-huai-of-chu">King Huai of Chu</a> who urged an alliance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, was slandered and exiled, and drowned himself in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miluo-river">Miluo River</a> when the Qin general Bo Qi sacked the Chu capital Ying in 278 BCE. Dragon boat races are traditionally said to commemorate his death. His name appears in no text before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The standard version has seventeen main sections. It opens with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-sao">Li Sao</a>, a poem of 373 verses that critics have often read as political allegory, though it also has religious and mythological aspects. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-songs">Jiu Ge</a>, or Nine Songs, in fact has eleven parts and seems to reflect shamanistic drama of the Yangzi River valley, with the invocation of divine beings through a process of courtship. The Tian Wen, or Heavenly Questions, consists of 172 questions on myth and ancient religious belief, with few answers.</p><p>Some pieces follow the four-syllable lines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry">Classic of Poetry</a>, others the long lines of the sao style, which imitates the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-sao">Li Sao</a>. The poems are a major source for the myths of Nüwa and the Great Flood and the Xiang River goddesses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Wang Yi, a librarian of the 2nd century CE under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shun-emperor">Emperor Shun</a> of Han, gave the anthology its present contents and added a commentary and his own Nine Longings as the last section. Liu Xiang had apparently compiled the poems earlier, probably from a compilation by Liu An. The traditional view held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qu-yuan">Qu Yuan</a> wrote about half the pieces, but modern scholars have no consensus on which are his. The Chu Ci never became a canonical work like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry">Classic of Poetry</a>, but it influenced poets including Jia Yi, Du Fu, Han Yu and Su Shi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chu Ci” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religion was rich in shamanistic elements: spirit mediums communicated with deities of rivers, mountains and the sky, as reflected in the Nine Songs of the <b>Songs of Chu</b>, and the Chu Silk Manuscript from Zidanku in Changsha describes cosmology and the spirits of the months.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Both readings correspond loosely to the 54-character Chinese version but have nothing matching its third and ninth lines, which Zhengzhang suggests were added to fit the <b>Chu Ci</b> poetic style.</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-the-yue-boatman">Song of the Yue Boatman</a></li></ul></section>
